#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ocu-nvcc: a CUDA compiler driver with nvcc's documented command line.

    ocu-nvcc [--ocu-target=cpu|nvidia|opencl|intel] [nvcc options] inputs...

Build systems call it where they would call nvcc: compile (-c), preprocess
(-E), dependencies (-M, -MD, -MF, -MT), link (default), shared libraries
(-shared), static libraries (-lib). The backend comes from --ocu-target, or
the name the driver is called by (ocu-nvcc-cpu, ocu-nvcc-nvidia), or the
environment variable OCU_TARGET, or "cpu":

  cpu     .cu files go through the CPU path of compiler/probe/ocu_cc.py
          (preprocess with our cuda_runtime.h, rewrite launches, compile as
          C++); the program runs on the CPU backend.
  nvidia  .cu files become PTX in our container plus a host object, built
          by LLVM Clang's CUDA mode; the program runs on an NVIDIA GPU
          through the installed driver.

.c/.cpp files go to the host compiler with our headers on the include
path. Linking adds our runtime libraries (ocu_cudart and what it needs).

The host and device compiler is LLVM Clang (default: the newest
/usr/lib/llvm-N/bin/clang++, else clang++, else c++); -ccbin picks another
host compiler. --version says so; build systems that identify compilers by
their --version output (CMake) therefore see a Clang-based CUDA compiler,
which is what this is.

GPU architecture options (-arch, -code, -gencode, --cuda-gpu-arch) are
recorded. The nvidia target builds PTX for the lowest one Clang supports;
the cpu target needs none. Options for NVIDIA's own back-end tools
(-Xptxas, -Xnvlink, -Xfatbin, -Xcudafe) are accepted and ignored.

No vendor SDK file is ever read: include and library paths inside a vendor
SDK are refused, as in ocu_cc.py.

cpu target: when the toolkit's lib folder has the whole-block pass
(libocu_cpu_loops.so, compiler/cpu_loops), kernels without synchronisation
points also get a loop entry (docs/cpu-region-loops.md); .cu files are then
compiled by the Clang the pass was built for. OCU_CPU_LOOPS=off turns it off,
OCU_CPU_LOOPS=<plugin> picks another.
"""

